issue_context.body:
I have been facing dificults to create access to elements from below structure:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/7027887/93387399-8d91eb80-f83f-11ea-9c7a-e07f78d34601.png)

```
&nvic_mux {
	uart5_mux: uart5_mux {
		maps = <&uart5 8>;
	};
	uart6_mux: uart6_mux {
		maps = <&uart6 9>;
	};
	uart7_mux: uart7_mux {
		maps = <&uart7 3>;
	};
};

&uart5 {
	status = "okay";
	current-speed = <115200>;

	#map-cells = <1>;
};

```
I noted that IRQ_CONNECT needs a const value. Currently I not found a way to evaluate the children from nvic_mux. The DT_INST_FOREACH_CHILD doesn't have a way to pass a CONST ARG. I'm not sure if an alternative with ARG will be enough because need a IF ELSE structure. Others alternatives need the child count and I'm not sure how get it.

My problem exists because on the dual core SoC the Cortex-M4 is directly connected to any peripheral and Cortex-M0+ NVIC have a MUX before it. User MUST configure up to 32 interrupts between a range that can be up to 240 interrupts sources. So, my solution was create a place like nvic_mux to map between peripherals and Cortex-M0. At driver level, Cortex-M4 will use the interrupt indicated on the peripheral itself. For Cortex-M0, user will choose a NVIC vector line [1-32] and mux it with the IRQ number from peripheral. As a result, the driver itself can have different IRQ line values for same peripheral. I wrote a table using DT_INST_FOREACH_CHILD but IRQ_CONNECT doesn't accept a variable. I can have other way to select that. However, on my view, it will be better have a place like nvic_mux to define the irq line and connect to peripheral. It will be easy to user see the vectors and change priorities, for instance.

Anyone can help to figure out best way to handle above ugly situation?

CC @galak @ioannisg @mbolivar-nordic
